{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ApK1JYA9mtGuQGBmfCY6Ly","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# mobile-android — technique log (days 1-12; updated after every day)\n2\t\n3\tHow the lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n4\t- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n5\t  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n6\t- Every APK so far was the same ~12.7 KB fixture with 6 zip entries (d10 added `res/xml/network_security_config.xml` +\n7\t  `resources.arsc` = 8 entries, 12845 B; d11 back to 6): `AndroidManifest.xml`, `assets/huntress_puzzle.json`,\n8\t  `classes.dex`, `META-INF/FIXTURE-.{SF,RSA}`, `MANIFEST.MF`. One question, `answer` = `ANS-` + 16 uppercase hex.\n9\t- Each day has a new Android IPC/security THEME, written in the manifest `<meta-data android:name=\"huntress.mechanic\">`\n10\t  (exported Activity, permissive deep link, nested Intent, mutable PendingIntent, allowBackup, exported ContentProvider,\n11\t  JavaScript-interface origin trust, cleartext session transport, permissive X509 trust manager, Network Security Config\n12\t  exception, locally stored credential in SharedPreferences, client-side SQLite authorization state). The theme was\n13\t  flavour only: no day required exploiting it, even when the dex carried a matching stub class (d7 `WebViewBridge`,\n14\t  d8 `CleartextSession`, d9 `TrustEveryone`) or the prompt demanded a live action (\"replay it against the backend\"\n15\t  d8, \"exploit a challenge CA\" d9 -- no CA/cert/network_security_config/TrustManager code was shipped).\n16\t- What actually varied day to day was the `codec` of `huntress_puzzle.json` (plain, reverse, base64, xor+key, split,\n17\t  hex, caesar+key, reverse again on d8, base64 again on d9, split again on d10, xor+key again on d11, hex again on d12 -- codecs CAN repeat). Within one day every variant (single, TT, all Mastery\n18\t  sets) used the same codec. Keys re-roll per variant. A new codec name is possible any day: decode it from the first\n19\t  4 chars, which must become `ANS-`. The dex also holds the codec name as a `CODEC` string constant (d8, d9) and a\n20\t  `DAY-NN:<theme_id>:<mechanic>` string.\n21\t- In every APK, `.ChallengeActivity` builds a device-loopback URL `http://127.0.0.1:19090/mobile/attempt/<id>/evidence?\n22\t  case_id=..&client_secret=SECRET-..&proof=<answer>`. That backend is NOT reachable (it lives on the phone). The GM\n23\t  accepted the decoded puzzle value as the \"backend evidence code\" on all 12 days.\n24\t- Expect escalation (inference, see reference/HARDENING.md): the answer may move into the dex, resources or a decrypt\n25\t  routine. `lib.android.extract_answer` has the fallback chain for that case.\n26\t\n27\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n28\t- **Puzzle codec** -- the same within one day, but `key` (xor byte d4, d11: 81 single/109 TT/205,29,44 Mastery; caesar shift d7: 10/18/9/3/4) and any\n29\t  split/offset parameters re-roll per variant: read them from `huntress_puzzle.json` every time. Keyless codecs\n30\t  (d8 `reverse`, d9 `base64`, 7 variants each) differed only in `data`. Rule: decode by declared codec, regex-check, else `decode_any`.\n31\t- **Per-variant identity** -- case ref, package suffix `v<12 hex>`, `client_secret` and the loopback attempt slug\n32\t  change per variant (all Mastery sets shared one slug on d7-d9, e.g. `d09-92be5cd6e8a5594f`; single and TT each\n33\t  had their own). APK size stays 12704 B, 6 entries. None of these feed the answer.\n34\t- **Mastery packaging** -- `delivery N: <name>.apk` per set, often identically named: pair by sha256. Set count\n35\t  varies: 3 sets (d1-d7, d10-d12), 5 sets on d8 and d9 (`set1..set5.answer`). Loop over the contract's answer_ids, never assume 3.\n36\t- **Answer location** -- so far always the puzzle JSON; if it decodes to nothing, fall back to dex/resources\n37\t  (`lib.android.extract_answer` chain) rather than assuming the sample's location.\n38\t\n39\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n40\t\n41\t| You see | First move |\n42\t|---|---|\n43\t| `*.apk` artifact, answer_format `ANS- + 16 uppercase hex` | `unzip -l x.apk`, then `unzip -p x.apk assets/huntress_puzzle.json` (takes seconds; it held the answer all 12 days). triage.md pre-extracts it to `work/extracted/` |\n44\t| puzzle JSON `{\"codec\":..,\"data\":..}` | decode per codec (table below). Check the result fullmatches `^ANS-[0-9A-F]{16}$` before you submit |\n45\t| Codec name not in the table below (d7 `caesar`) | compare `data[:4]` with `ANS-`: constant ord diff = shift, constant XOR = xor key. Implement, regex-check, submit |\n46\t| No puzzle JSON, or it decodes to no `ANS-` | `python -c 'from lib.android import extract_answer'` (zip scan, then dex strings incl. multidex, then apktool/jadx scan), then `strings -a classes*.dex \\| grep ANS-` |\n47\t| Need the theme, component, scheme or exported flags | `droidasc getmanifest x.apk` (fast). `aapt dump badging x.apk` / `aapt dump xmltree x.apk AndroidManifest.xml` (installed). Also `lib.android.manifest_facts(apk)` |\n48\t| Need handler logic (URL built, extras read, constants) | `droidasc findrefs x.apk string <scheme or URL>`, then `droidasc getclass x.apk <pkg>.ChallengeActivity`; whole app: `jadx -d work/jadx x.apk` |\n49\t| Need resources or strings.xml / smali | `apktool d -f -o work/apktool x.apk` |\n50\t| Loopback `127.0.0.1:19090` in the code | do NOT chase it. It is device-local (refused on Homebase, times out elsewhere). The answer is static |\n51\t| Prompt says \"recover the session from cleartext traffic and replay it against the backend\" (d8) | still static: no pcap/HAR/network_security_config in the APK; the \"traffic\" is the `http://` loopback URL and `usesCleartextTraffic=true`. Decode the asset, submit |\n52\t| Prompt says \"exploit a challenge CA accepted by a permissive trust implementation\" (d9) | still static: no `.pem`/`.crt`/`res/raw`, no `network_security_config`, dex has no `X509TrustManager`/`SSLContext` refs, only a stub `TrustEveryone` class. Decode the asset, submit. A real TLS task would ship a cert or MITM target -- check `unzip -l` first |\n53\t| Native `lib/*.so` inside the APK (not seen yet) | route to the binary-re skill (rizin / Ghidra headless) |\n54\t| Backup theme with a real `.ab` file (not seen yet) | Android backup = 24-byte header + zlib stream: `dd bs=24 skip=1 \\| zlib-flate -uncompress \\| tar x` (or abe.jar) |\n55\t| Mastery artifact names like `delivery N: foo_mastery_0N.apk` | strip the `delivery N: ` prefix to find the file (`lib.common.find_artifact` does this). Ids are `setN.answer` |\n56\t\n57\tPuzzle codecs seen (`schema: huntress.mobile.puzzle.v1`):\n58\t- `plain`: data is the answer.\n59\t- `reverse`: reverse the string (`...-SNA` -> `ANS-...`).\n60\t- `base64` (d3, d9): `base64 -d` (`QU5TLTNEM0I4MDM5NTI0MEI4RUU=` -> `ANS-3D3B80395240B8EE`).\n61\t- `hex`: `xxd -r -p`.\n62\t- `caesar` (d7): `{data, key:int}`. Plain ASCII codepoint shift: `chr(ord(c) - key)` for EVERY char (digits and `-`\n63\t  shift too, e.g. `KX]7` key 10 -> `ANS-`). A letters-only mod-26 ROT is wrong. Derive the sign from `data[:4]` vs `ANS-`.\n64\t- `xor`: data is base64. b64-decode, then XOR every byte with the int `key` (0-255, changes per variant).\n65\t- `split`: `parts[]` + `order[]`, where `order[i]` is the OUTPUT SLOT of `parts[i]` (not `parts[order[i]]`). Try both\n66\t  readings and keep the one that fullmatches `ANS-`.\n67\t- The solvers also handle base32, rot13 and a `lib.decode.peel` fallback, ready for new codecs.\n68\t  `lib.android.decode_payload` now covers every codec above incl. caesar/reverse, `puzzle_candidates(apk)` also does\n69\t  split (both order readings), and `decode_any` is the name-agnostic fallback. Call them instead of re-typing a table.\n70\t- Unknown codec + int key: try the known-plaintext trick. `key_or_shift = f(data[:4], \"ANS-\")` (xor: byte ^; shift:\n71\t  ord diff). A constant diff across the 4 chars names the transform at once.\n72\t\n73\t## Day by day\n74\t\n75\t**Day 1 — Exported Trust** (\"exported Activity and caller trust\"): exported `.ChallengeActivity`, deep link\n76\t`huntress01://exported-trust/unlock`; `onCreate` reads `answer`/`case_id` extras and builds the loopback evidence URL.\n77\tCodec `plain`. Tools: `droidasc getmanifest`, apktool, jadx, reading the asset from the zip. About 4 min lost trying to\n78\treach `127.0.0.1:19090` via `hctf hb exec curl` (Homebase `ss -tln`, GM proxy, 10.0.0.219:19090 -- all refused/timed\n79\tout). CORRECT at about 5.4 min, first try. TT/Mastery windows were 30s on d1 (6-11s from d2 on).\n80\t\n81\t**Day 2 — Deep Link Drift** (\"permissive scheme, host, and path validation\"): same shape, no host/path checks. Codec\n82\t`reverse`. CORRECT at 33s. A harness re-start overwrote `single_solve/start.json` without `attempt_id` (restore from\n83\t`log.jsonl`). Mastery expired 8 times: 6s window with 3 downloads + submit inside it (fixed by Homebase prewarm).\n84\t\n85\t**Day 3 — Intent Injection** (\"attacker-controlled nested Intent\"): same shape. Codec `base64`. CORRECT at 18s.\n86\t\n87\t**Day 4 — Pending Intent Relay** (\"mutable PendingIntent relay\"): same shape. Codec `xor` `{data:<b64>, key:<int>}`;\n88\tkey re-rolled per variant (103, 189, 31, 81). About 30s lost on the loopback. CORRECT at 77s.\n89\t\n90\t**Day 5 — Backup Spill** (`allowBackup=\"true\"`, no backup file shipped): codec `split` (`parts` + `order`); the\n91\t`parts[order[i]]` reading was a dead end, `order[i]` = output slot of `parts[i]` was right. CORRECT at 27s. TT/Mastery\n92\t7s window (elapsed 5.3s: start 1.8, download 2.3, solve 0.08, submit 1.2).\n93\t\n94\t**Day 6 — Provider Paths** (exported `<provider ... authorities=<pkg>.evidence grantUriPermissions=true>`, `query()`\n95\ta stub returning null): codec `hex`. About 2.3 min lost on the loopback and grepping hctf/ for a backend route.\n96\tCORRECT at about 3 min.\n97\t\n98\t**Day 7 — WebView Bridge** (mechanic \"JavaScript interface origin trust\"; prompt \"Cross a challenge-owned WebView\n99\tbridge through an untrusted origin ... Submit only the exact backend answer code\")\n100\t- Same fixture (12704 B, 6 entries). Deep link `huntress07://webview-bridge` pathPrefix `/unlock` on exported\n101\t  `.ChallengeActivity`; dex adds a `WebViewBridge` class (`describe`/`encode`/`getData`) that was a stub. Same loopback\n102\t  evidence URL. All static, no backend.\n103\t- New codec `caesar`, key 10: ASCII shift of every char by `-key`. Spotted by eye in one step (`K`-10=`A`,\n104\t  `]`-10=`S`, `7`-10=`-`).\n105\t- Worker flow: read triage.md + pre-extracted `assets/huntress_puzzle.json`, wrote the caesar branch into solve.py,\n106\t  `hctf submit --from-solver`. No loopback chase, no decompile. 5 tool calls.\n107\t- Start 13:00:10Z -> CORRECT 13:01:02Z = 52s, first try. TT (9s window) correct in 3.5s, key 18. Mastery (10s\n108\t  window) correct in 2.3s, keys 9/3/4. Zero wrong submissions.\n109\t\n110\t**Day 8 — Cleartext Session** (mechanic \"cleartext session transport\"; prompt \"Recover a synthetic session from allowed\n111\tcleartext traffic and replay it against the backend ... Submit only the exact backend answer code\")\n112\t- Same fixture (12704 B, 6 entries). Manifest: `usesCleartextTraffic=true`, `allowBackup=false`, INTERNET permission,\n113\t  deep link `huntress08://cleartext-session` pathPrefix `/unlock` on exported `.ChallengeActivity`. Dex: stub class\n114\t  `CleartextSession` (`describe`/`encode`/`getData`), `CODEC`=\"reverse\", the usual `http://127.0.0.1:19090/mobile/\n115\t  attempt/d08-<16 hex>/evidence?case_id=..&client_secret=SECRET-..&proof=` URL. No captured traffic, no session token\n116\t  file, no network config: the \"cleartext session\" is that URL. Nothing was replayed.\n117\t- Codec `reverse` (repeat of d2): `\"6667D24E8BC6DE39-SNA\"` -> `ANS-93ED6CB8E42D7666`.\n118\t- Worker flow: read triage.md (pre-extracted puzzle JSON), pasted the d7 codec table into solve.py,\n119\t  `hctf submit --from-solver`. 2 tool calls, no decompile, no loopback chase.\n120\t- Start 15:43:54Z -> CORRECT 15:44:16Z = 22s, first try. TT (8s window) correct in 1.5s.\n121\t  Mastery (8s window, 5 sets) correct in 2.27s. Zero wrong submissions.\n122\t\n123\t**Day 9 — Trust Everyone** (mechanic \"permissive X509 trust manager\"; prompt \"Exploit a challenge CA ...\"): same 6-entry\n124\tfixture, stub `TrustEveryone`, no TLS code -- the \"CA\"/\"backend\" were narrative. Codec `base64` (repeat of d3) ->\n125\t`ANS-3D3B80395240B8EE`. 2 tool calls, ~25 s, first try; TT 3.16 s (cold imports), Mastery 5 sets 1.7 s.\n126\t\n127\t**Day 10 — Network Config Split** (mechanic \"Network Security Config exception\"): first fixture with extra entries --\n128\t`res/xml/network_security_config.xml` (cleartextTrafficPermitted=true for domain 127.0.0.1) + `resources.arsc`. Still\n129\tstatic: codec `split` (repeat of d5), `parts [\"56C4B5B0D\",\"ANS-4\",\"9D8953\"]`, `order [2,0,1]` = output slot of each part.\n130\tAll 3 modes CORRECT first try (TT 13 s / Mastery 10 s windows, 3 sets).\n131\t\n132\t**Day 11 — Local Secrets** (mechanic `DAY-11:shared_preferences:locally stored synthetic credential`; prompt \"Separate\n133\tactive credentials from decoys across generated local stores.\")\n134\t- Same 6-entry fixture (12704 B). No SharedPreferences XML, sqlite or other \"local store\" file shipped; the only\n135\t  credential-looking string besides the asset is the dex's loopback `client_secret=SECRET-<22 hex>` (a decoy, never\n136\t  the answer). Codec `xor` (repeat of d4): `{\"data\":\"EB8CfGkXY2NkZmVmYBUXZWkVYGU=\",\"key\":81}` -> b64-decode, XOR 81 ->\n137\t  `ANS-8F2257471DF48D14`.\n138\t- Worker (warm launch): one command decoded the triage-extracted JSON by hand (`base64 -d | xor 81`), a second filled\n139\t  `answer()` with `lib.android.puzzle_candidates` + `ANS-` regex (fallback `raw_entry_scan`/`dex_strings`) and\n140\t  submitted. Launch 01:55:40Z -> CORRECT 01:56:08Z = ~28 s, first try. TT (7 s window) 0.93 s in-window; Mastery (8 s, 3\n141\t  sets) 0.82 s. Zero wrong submissions.\n142\t\n143\t**Day 12 — SQLite Assumptions** (mechanic `DAY-12:sqlite_cache:client-side SQLite authorization state`; prompt\n144\t\"Reconstruct authorization from a synthetic SQLite cache and prove server-side impact.\")\n145\t- Same 6-entry fixture (12704 B), deep link `huntress12://sqlite-assumptions`, stub class `SQLiteAssumptions`. NO `.db`/sqlite\n146\t  file, no `SQLiteOpenHelper` code: the \"cache\" was narrative again. Codec `hex` (repeat of d6):\n147\t  `414e532d30333946304439303731313131303530` -> `ANS-039F0D9071111050`.\n148\t- Solved by the carried-over solver (`lib.android.puzzle_candidates` + `ANS-` regex) with no worker tool call: start\n149\t  23:10:48Z -> CORRECT 23:10:52.8Z (~4.5 s). TT 30 s window, in_window 1.49 s; Mastery 3 sets, in_window 0.95 s. 0 wrong.\n150\t\n151\t## GM answer conventions learned\n152\t- One field, `answer`. Single solve and TT use id `answer`; Mastery uses `setN.answer` (3 sets d1-d7, 5 on d8-d9), one\n153\t  APK per set.\n154\t- Format is `ANS-` + exactly 16 UPPERCASE hex, normalization `exact`. Uppercase the hex after decoding and add no\n155\t  whitespace.\n156\t- No mobile answer has ever been rejected (zero wrong submissions on days 1-12). `ANS-<16 uppercase hex>` confirmed\n157\t  again on d8 and d9 (7/7 variants each). The GM accepts the decoded\n158\t  `huntress_puzzle.json` value as the \"backend evidence code\" even though the prompt says \"invoke ... return the backend\n159\t  evidence code\".\n160\t- Mastery feedback is per set: `{\"result\":\"accepted\",\"remaining\":N}` after each set, then `CORRECT` on the last. An\n161\t  empty submit after the deadline returns `expired`.\n162\t- Prompt boilerplate repeats daily: \"For case MOB-NN-<10 hex> in package com.huntress.mobile.dNN.v<12 hex>\". The case\n163\t  ref and package suffix change per variant and were never needed for the answer.\n164\t\n165\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n166\t- **Chasing the loopback backend** was the only recurring time sink: about 4 min (d1), about 30s (d4) and about 2.3 min\n167\t  (d6). `127.0.0.1:19090` is the phone's own loopback. If the decoded asset already matches the answer_format, submit it.\n168\t- **split codec order semantics:** two plausible readings exist. Validate the candidates against the answer regex\n169\t  instead of guessing.\n170\t- **xor codec:** `data` is base64 first, and `key` may vary per variant (int; the solvers also accept a numeric string\n171\t  or a repeating string key).\n172\t- **caesar codec:** the shift is over raw codepoints, not the alphabet (`data` contains `]`, `` ` ``, `<`, `>` --\n173\t  JSON-escaped as `\\u003c`/`\\u003e`, so json-load it, never regex the raw text). Keep only the candidate that\n174\t  fullmatches `^ANS-[0-9A-F]{16}$`.\n175\t- **Mastery filenames** carry a literal `delivery N: ` prefix. A naive path lookup fails.\n176\t- **6-11s timed windows** (day 2+; d7 TT 9s/Mastery 10s, d8 both 8s, d9 TT 8s/Mastery 11s) are dominated by start + download + submit. Solve time is about 0.1s, so do no\n177\t  decompiling in-window: read the zip entry directly. Day 2 Mastery expired 8 times before Homebase routing.\n178\t- **Don't decompile when you don't need to.** Every solve so far (d1-d12) needed only the asset; the manifest was context only.\n179\t  Keep jadx/apktool for the day the asset is missing or lacks `ANS-`.\n180\t- **Prompt wording escalates, the artifact did not** (d7 \"cross a bridge\", d8 \"replay the session against the backend\",\n181\t  d9 \"exploit a challenge CA\", d11 \"separate active credentials from decoys\", d12 \"reconstruct authorization from a SQLite cache and prove server-side impact\"): judge by what the APK contains (pcap/HAR, token file, `.db`/sqlite, cert, network_security_config,\n182\t  native lib), not by the verb.\n183\t- **Hand-typed codec tables:** each day's solver re-pasted the decode table; it worked, but `lib.android` already\n184\t  covers it -- use `puzzle_candidates`/`decode_payload` so a missed branch can't cost a timed run.\n185\t- **Harness, not GM:** a re-start can clobber `single_solve/start.json` (no attempt_id), which makes a solved lane look\n186\t  incomplete. Check `hctf status` before re-solving.\n187\t- Watch for the cheap escalation (hypothesis in reference/HARDENING.md, not yet seen): answer moved into the dex,\n188\t  `strings.xml`, BuildConfig or a native lib, or computed by a decrypt routine. Then trace `ChallengeActivity` and run\n189\t  `lib.android.extract_answer`.\n190\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00ed06c6-0522-49a2-88e5-afcd48f88547","uuid":"18f5f43c-c0fc-4ba3-8f5f-7ad0dc2bbb93","timestamp":"2026-10-13T13:06:02.902Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/mobile-android.md","content":"# mobile-android — technique log (days 1-12; updated after every day)\n\nHow the lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Every APK so far was the same ~12.7 KB fixture with 6 zip entries (d10 added `res/xml/network_security_config.xml` +\n  `resources.arsc` = 8 entries, 12845 B; d11 back to 6): `AndroidManifest.xml`, `assets/huntress_puzzle.json`,\n  `classes.dex`, `META-INF/FIXTURE-.{SF,RSA}`, `MANIFEST.MF`. One question, `answer` = `ANS-` + 16 uppercase hex.\n- Each day has a new Android IPC/security THEME, written in the manifest `<meta-data android:name=\"huntress.mechanic\">`\n  (exported Activity, permissive deep link, nested Intent, mutable PendingIntent, allowBackup, exported ContentProvider,\n  JavaScript-interface origin trust, cleartext session transport, permissive X509 trust manager, Network Security Config\n  exception, locally stored credential in SharedPreferences, client-side SQLite authorization state). The theme was\n  flavour only: no day required exploiting it, even when the dex carried a matching stub class (d7 `WebViewBridge`,\n  d8 `CleartextSession`, d9 `TrustEveryone`) or the prompt demanded a live action (\"replay it against the backend\"\n  d8, \"exploit a challenge CA\" d9 -- no CA/cert/network_security_config/TrustManager code was shipped).\n- What actually varied day to day was the `codec` of `huntress_puzzle.json` (plain, reverse, base64, xor+key, split,\n  hex, caesar+key, reverse again on d8, base64 again on d9, split again on d10, xor+key again on d11, hex again on d12 -- codecs CAN repeat). Within one day every variant (single, TT, all Mastery\n  sets) used the same codec. Keys re-roll per variant. A new codec name is possible any day: decode it from the first\n  4 chars, which must become `ANS-`. The dex also holds the codec name as a `CODEC` string constant (d8, d9) and a\n  `DAY-NN:<theme_id>:<mechanic>` string.\n- In every APK, `.ChallengeActivity` builds a device-loopback URL `http://127.0.0.1:19090/mobile/attempt/<id>/evidence?\n  case_id=..&client_secret=SECRET-..&proof=<answer>`. That backend is NOT reachable (it lives on the phone). The GM\n  accepted the decoded puzzle value as the \"backend evidence code\" on all 12 days.\n- Expect escalation (inference, see reference/HARDENING.md): the answer may move into the dex, resources or a decrypt\n  routine. `lib.android.extract_answer` has the fallback chain for that case.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Puzzle codec** -- the same within one day, but `key` (xor byte d4, d11: 81 single/109 TT/205,29,44 Mastery; caesar shift d7: 10/18/9/3/4) and any\n  split/offset parameters re-roll per variant: read them from `huntress_puzzle.json` every time. Keyless codecs\n  (d8 `reverse`, d9 `base64`, 7 variants each) differed only in `data`. Rule: decode by declared codec, regex-check, else `decode_any`.\n- **Per-variant identity** -- case ref, package suffix `v<12 hex>`, `client_secret` and the loopback attempt slug\n  change per variant (all Mastery sets shared one slug on d7-d9, e.g. `d09-92be5cd6e8a5594f`; single and TT each\n  had their own). APK size stays 12704 B, 6 entries. None of these feed the answer.\n- **Mastery packaging** -- `delivery N: <name>.apk` per set, often identically named: pair by sha256. Set count\n  varies: 3 sets (d1-d7, d10-d12), 5 sets on d8 and d9 (`set1..set5.answer`). Loop over the contract's answer_ids, never assume 3.\n- **Answer location** -- so far always the puzzle JSON; if it decodes to nothing, fall back to dex/resources\n  (`lib.android.extract_answer` chain) rather than assuming the sample's location.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n\n| You see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `*.apk` artifact, answer_format `ANS- + 16 uppercase hex` | `unzip -l x.apk`, then `unzip -p x.apk assets/huntress_puzzle.json` (takes seconds; it held the answer all 12 days). triage.md pre-extracts it to `work/extracted/` |\n| puzzle JSON `{\"codec\":..,\"data\":..}` | decode per codec (table below). Check the result fullmatches `^ANS-[0-9A-F]{16}$` before you submit |\n| Codec name not in the table below (d7 `caesar`) | compare `data[:4]` with `ANS-`: constant ord diff = shift, constant XOR = xor key. Implement, regex-check, submit |\n| No puzzle JSON, or it decodes to no `ANS-` | `python -c 'from lib.android import extract_answer'` (zip scan, then dex strings incl. multidex, then apktool/jadx scan), then `strings -a classes*.dex \\| grep ANS-` |\n| Need the theme, component, scheme or exported flags | `droidasc getmanifest x.apk` (fast). `aapt dump badging x.apk` / `aapt dump xmltree x.apk AndroidManifest.xml` (installed). Also `lib.android.manifest_facts(apk)` |\n| Need handler logic (URL built, extras read, constants) | `droidasc findrefs x.apk string <scheme or URL>`, then `droidasc getclass x.apk <pkg>.ChallengeActivity`; whole app: `jadx -d work/jadx x.apk` |\n| Need resources or strings.xml / smali | `apktool d -f -o work/apktool x.apk` |\n| Loopback `127.0.0.1:19090` in the code | do NOT chase it. It is device-local (refused on Homebase, times out elsewhere). The answer is static |\n| Prompt says \"recover the session from cleartext traffic and replay it against the backend\" (d8) | still static: no pcap/HAR/network_security_config in the APK; the \"traffic\" is the `http://` loopback URL and `usesCleartextTraffic=true`. Decode the asset, submit |\n| Prompt says \"exploit a challenge CA accepted by a permissive trust implementation\" (d9) | still static: no `.pem`/`.crt`/`res/raw`, no `network_security_config`, dex has no `X509TrustManager`/`SSLContext` refs, only a stub `TrustEveryone` class. Decode the asset, submit. A real TLS task would ship a cert or MITM target -- check `unzip -l` first |\n| Native `lib/*.so` inside the APK (not seen yet) | route to the binary-re skill (rizin / Ghidra headless) |\n| Backup theme with a real `.ab` file (not seen yet) | Android backup = 24-byte header + zlib stream: `dd bs=24 skip=1 \\| zlib-flate -uncompress \\| tar x` (or abe.jar) |\n| Mastery artifact names like `delivery N: foo_mastery_0N.apk` | strip the `delivery N: ` prefix to find the file (`lib.common.find_artifact` does this). Ids are `setN.answer` |\n\nPuzzle codecs seen (`schema: huntress.mobile.puzzle.v1`):\n- `plain`: data is the answer.\n- `reverse`: reverse the string (`...-SNA` -> `ANS-...`).\n- `base64` (d3, d9): `base64 -d` (`QU5TLTNEM0I4MDM5NTI0MEI4RUU=` -> `ANS-3D3B80395240B8EE`).\n- `hex`: `xxd -r -p`.\n- `caesar` (d7): `{data, key:int}`. Plain ASCII codepoint shift: `chr(ord(c) - key)` for EVERY char (digits and `-`\n  shift too, e.g. `KX]7` key 10 -> `ANS-`). A letters-only mod-26 ROT is wrong. Derive the sign from `data[:4]` vs `ANS-`.\n- `xor`: data is base64. b64-decode, then XOR every byte with the int `key` (0-255, changes per variant).\n- `split`: `parts[]` + `order[]`, where `order[i]` is the OUTPUT SLOT of `parts[i]` (not `parts[order[i]]`). Try both\n  readings and keep the one that fullmatches `ANS-`.\n- The solvers also handle base32, rot13 and a `lib.decode.peel` fallback, ready for new codecs.\n  `lib.android.decode_payload` now covers every codec above incl. caesar/reverse, `puzzle_candidates(apk)` also does\n  split (both order readings), and `decode_any` is the name-agnostic fallback. Call them instead of re-typing a table.\n- Unknown codec + int key: try the known-plaintext trick. `key_or_shift = f(data[:4], \"ANS-\")` (xor: byte ^; shift:\n  ord diff). A constant diff across the 4 chars names the transform at once.\n\n## Day by day\n\n**Day 1 — Exported Trust** (\"exported Activity and caller trust\"): exported `.ChallengeActivity`, deep link\n`huntress01://exported-trust/unlock`; `onCreate` reads `answer`/`case_id` extras and builds the loopback evidence URL.\nCodec `plain`. Tools: `droidasc getmanifest`, apktool, jadx, reading the asset from the zip. About 4 min lost trying to\nreach `127.0.0.1:19090` via `hctf hb exec curl` (Homebase `ss -tln`, GM proxy, 10.0.0.219:19090 -- all refused/timed\nout). CORRECT at about 5.4 min, first try. TT/Mastery windows were 30s on d1 (6-11s from d2 on).\n\n**Day 2 — Deep Link Drift** (\"permissive scheme, host, and path validation\"): same shape, no host/path checks. Codec\n`reverse`. CORRECT at 33s. A harness re-start overwrote `single_solve/start.json` without `attempt_id` (restore from\n`log.jsonl`). Mastery expired 8 times: 6s window with 3 downloads + submit inside it (fixed by Homebase prewarm).\n\n**Day 3 — Intent Injection** (\"attacker-controlled nested Intent\"): same shape. Codec `base64`. CORRECT at 18s.\n\n**Day 4 — Pending Intent Relay** (\"mutable PendingIntent relay\"): same shape. Codec `xor` `{data:<b64>, key:<int>}`;\nkey re-rolled per variant (103, 189, 31, 81). About 30s lost on the loopback. CORRECT at 77s.\n\n**Day 5 — Backup Spill** (`allowBackup=\"true\"`, no backup file shipped): codec `split` (`parts` + `order`); the\n`parts[order[i]]` reading was a dead end, `order[i]` = output slot of `parts[i]` was right. CORRECT at 27s. TT/Mastery\n7s window (elapsed 5.3s: start 1.8, download 2.3, solve 0.08, submit 1.2).\n\n**Day 6 — Provider Paths** (exported `<provider ... authorities=<pkg>.evidence grantUriPermissions=true>`, `query()`\na stub returning null): codec `hex`. About 2.3 min lost on the loopback and grepping hctf/ for a backend route.\nCORRECT at about 3 min.\n\n**Day 7 — WebView Bridge** (mechanic \"JavaScript interface origin trust\"; prompt \"Cross a challenge-owned WebView\nbridge through an untrusted origin ... Submit only the exact backend answer code\")\n- Same fixture (12704 B, 6 entries). Deep link `huntress07://webview-bridge` pathPrefix `/unlock` on exported\n  `.ChallengeActivity`; dex adds a `WebViewBridge` class (`describe`/`encode`/`getData`) that was a stub. Same loopback\n  evidence URL. All static, no backend.\n- New codec `caesar`, key 10: ASCII shift of every char by `-key`. Spotted by eye in one step (`K`-10=`A`,\n  `]`-10=`S`, `7`-10=`-`).\n- Worker flow: read triage.md + pre-extracted `assets/huntress_puzzle.json`, wrote the caesar branch into solve.py,\n  `hctf submit --from-solver`. No loopback chase, no decompile. 5 tool calls.\n- Start 13:00:10Z -> CORRECT 13:01:02Z = 52s, first try. TT (9s window) correct in 3.5s, key 18. Mastery (10s\n  window) correct in 2.3s, keys 9/3/4. Zero wrong submissions.\n\n**Day 8 — Cleartext Session** (mechanic \"cleartext session transport\"; prompt \"Recover a synthetic session from allowed\ncleartext traffic and replay it against the backend ... Submit only the exact backend answer code\")\n- Same fixture (12704 B, 6 entries). Manifest: `usesCleartextTraffic=true`, `allowBackup=false`, INTERNET permission,\n  deep link `huntress08://cleartext-session` pathPrefix `/unlock` on exported `.ChallengeActivity`. Dex: stub class\n  `CleartextSession` (`describe`/`encode`/`getData`), `CODEC`=\"reverse\", the usual `http://127.0.0.1:19090/mobile/\n  attempt/d08-<16 hex>/evidence?case_id=..&client_secret=SECRET-..&proof=` URL. No captured traffic, no session token\n  file, no network config: the \"cleartext session\" is that URL. Nothing was replayed.\n- Codec `reverse` (repeat of d2): `\"6667D24E8BC6DE39-SNA\"` -> `ANS-93ED6CB8E42D7666`.\n- Worker flow: read triage.md (pre-extracted puzzle JSON), pasted the d7 codec table into solve.py,\n  `hctf submit --from-solver`. 2 tool calls, no decompile, no loopback chase.\n- Start 15:43:54Z -> CORRECT 15:44:16Z = 22s, first try. TT (8s window) correct in 1.5s.\n  Mastery (8s window, 5 sets) correct in 2.27s. Zero wrong submissions.\n\n**Day 9 — Trust Everyone** (mechanic \"permissive X509 trust manager\"; prompt \"Exploit a challenge CA ...\"): same 6-entry\nfixture, stub `TrustEveryone`, no TLS code -- the \"CA\"/\"backend\" were narrative. Codec `base64` (repeat of d3) ->\n`ANS-3D3B80395240B8EE`. 2 tool calls, ~25 s, first try; TT 3.16 s (cold imports), Mastery 5 sets 1.7 s.\n\n**Day 10 — Network Config Split** (mechanic \"Network Security Config exception\"): first fixture with extra entries --\n`res/xml/network_security_config.xml` (cleartextTrafficPermitted=true for domain 127.0.0.1) + `resources.arsc`. Still\nstatic: codec `split` (repeat of d5), `parts [\"56C4B5B0D\",\"ANS-4\",\"9D8953\"]`, `order [2,0,1]` = output slot of each part.\nAll 3 modes CORRECT first try (TT 13 s / Mastery 10 s windows, 3 sets).\n\n**Day 11 — Local Secrets** (mechanic `DAY-11:shared_preferences:locally stored synthetic credential`; prompt \"Separate\nactive credentials from decoys across generated local stores.\")\n- Same 6-entry fixture (12704 B). No SharedPreferences XML, sqlite or other \"local store\" file shipped; the only\n  credential-looking string besides the asset is the dex's loopback `client_secret=SECRET-<22 hex>` (a decoy, never\n  the answer). Codec `xor` (repeat of d4): `{\"data\":\"EB8CfGkXY2NkZmVmYBUXZWkVYGU=\",\"key\":81}` -> b64-decode, XOR 81 ->\n  `ANS-8F2257471DF48D14`.\n- Worker (warm launch): one command decoded the triage-extracted JSON by hand (`base64 -d | xor 81`), a second filled\n  `answer()` with `lib.android.puzzle_candidates` + `ANS-` regex (fallback `raw_entry_scan`/`dex_strings`) and\n  submitted. Launch 01:55:40Z -> CORRECT 01:56:08Z = ~28 s, first try. TT (7 s window) 0.93 s in-window; Mastery (8 s, 3\n  sets) 0.82 s. Zero wrong submissions.\n\n**Day 12 — SQLite Assumptions** (mechanic `DAY-12:sqlite_cache:client-side SQLite authorization state`; prompt\n\"Reconstruct authorization from a synthetic SQLite cache and prove server-side impact.\")\n- Same 6-entry fixture (12704 B), deep link `huntress12://sqlite-assumptions`, stub class `SQLiteAssumptions`. NO `.db`/sqlite\n  file, no `SQLiteOpenHelper` code: the \"cache\" was narrative again. Codec `hex` (repeat of d6):\n  `414e532d30333946304439303731313131303530` -> `ANS-039F0D9071111050`.\n- Solved by the carried-over solver (`lib.android.puzzle_candidates` + `ANS-` regex) with no worker tool call: start\n  23:10:48Z -> CORRECT 23:10:52.8Z (~4.5 s). TT 30 s window, in_window 1.49 s; Mastery 3 sets, in_window 0.95 s. 0 wrong.\n\n## GM answer conventions learned\n- One field, `answer`. Single solve and TT use id `answer`; Mastery uses `setN.answer` (3 sets d1-d7, 5 on d8-d9), one\n  APK per set.\n- Format is `ANS-` + exactly 16 UPPERCASE hex, normalization `exact`. Uppercase the hex after decoding and add no\n  whitespace.\n- No mobile answer has ever been rejected (zero wrong submissions on days 1-12). `ANS-<16 uppercase hex>` confirmed\n  again on d8 and d9 (7/7 variants each). The GM accepts the decoded\n  `huntress_puzzle.json` value as the \"backend evidence code\" even though the prompt says \"invoke ... return the backend\n  evidence code\".\n- Mastery feedback is per set: `{\"result\":\"accepted\",\"remaining\":N}` after each set, then `CORRECT` on the last. An\n  empty submit after the deadline returns `expired`.\n- Prompt boilerplate repeats daily: \"For case MOB-NN-<10 hex> in package com.huntress.mobile.dNN.v<12 hex>\". The case\n  ref and package suffix change per variant and were never needed for the answer.\n\n## Pitfalls that cost time\n- **Chasing the loopback backend** was the only recurring time sink: about 4 min (d1), about 30s (d4) and about 2.3 min\n  (d6). `127.0.0.1:19090` is the phone's own loopback. If the decoded asset already matches the answer_format, submit it.\n- **split codec order semantics:** two plausible readings exist. Validate the candidates against the answer regex\n  instead of guessing.\n- **xor codec:** `data` is base64 first, and `key` may vary per variant (int; the solvers also accept a numeric string\n  or a repeating string key).\n- **caesar codec:** the shift is over raw codepoints, not the alphabet (`data` contains `]`, `` ` ``, `<`, `>` --\n  JSON-escaped as `\\u003c`/`\\u003e`, so json-load it, never regex the raw text). Keep only the candidate that\n  fullmatches `^ANS-[0-9A-F]{16}$`.\n- **Mastery filenames** carry a literal `delivery N: ` prefix. A naive path lookup fails.\n- **6-11s timed windows** (day 2+; d7 TT 9s/Mastery 10s, d8 both 8s, d9 TT 8s/Mastery 11s) are dominated by start + download + submit. Solve time is about 0.1s, so do no\n  decompiling in-window: read the zip entry directly. Day 2 Mastery expired 8 times before Homebase routing.\n- **Don't decompile when you don't need to.** Every solve so far (d1-d12) needed only the asset; the manifest was context only.\n  Keep jadx/apktool for the day the asset is missing or lacks `ANS-`.\n- **Prompt wording escalates, the artifact did not** (d7 \"cross a bridge\", d8 \"replay the session against the backend\",\n  d9 \"exploit a challenge CA\", d11 \"separate active credentials from decoys\", d12 \"reconstruct authorization from a SQLite cache and prove server-side impact\"): judge by what the APK contains (pcap/HAR, token file, `.db`/sqlite, cert, network_security_config,\n  native lib), not by the verb.\n- **Hand-typed codec tables:** each day's solver re-pasted the decode table; it worked, but `lib.android` already\n  covers it -- use `puzzle_candidates`/`decode_payload` so a missed branch can't cost a timed run.\n- **Harness, not GM:** a re-start can clobber `single_solve/start.json` (no attempt_id), which makes a solved lane look\n  incomplete. Check `hctf status` before re-solving.\n- Watch for the cheap escalation (hypothesis in reference/HARDENING.md, not yet seen): answer moved into the dex,\n  `strings.xml`, BuildConfig or a native lib, or computed by a decrypt routine. Then trace `ChallengeActivity` and run\n  `lib.android.extract_answer`.\n","numLines":190,"startLine":1,"totalLines":190}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01ApK1JYA9mtGuQGBmfCY6Ly","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
