{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ae8238ff506183723","run_id":"0mv6p0onz-2a538d88","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RrJC7yyJPZqNngCxuN3rEe","status":"completed","output_file":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/.run/tmp/claude-1000/-home-testpwn-huntress-2026/00ed06c6-0522-49a2-88e5-afcd48f88547/tasks/ae8238ff506183723.output","summary":"I checked the solver in memory against all three of today's cases. Day 13 needed no new code: the solver carried over from earlier days (`solve.py` dated 07:11 local, before the 21:00 local start) answered every d13 field correctly on the first try. No d13 answer was ever rejected.\n\n**(1) The artifact**\n- The download was `evidence.zip` (32,241,637 B, WinZip AES, password `infected`) with 14 members. The new one is `d13--variant-000.elf`, 749,208 B: an x86-64 ELF64 EXEC, dynamically linked, not stripped.\n- **Crystal fingerprint:** the strings contain `Crystal:H` (11 hits) and the Boehm GC (libgc) exports (`GC_debug_malloc`, `GC_world_stopped`, ...), and `nm` matches \"crystal\" 168 times.\n- **Where the config lived:** a base64 Crystal String literal in `.rodata` at file offset 560188 (0x88c3c), starting `SENGRwGRcAFh...` (508 chars). It sits right after Crystal's 12-byte String header `01000000 fc010000 fc010000` (type_id=1, bytesize=508, length=508).\n- **Encoding:** base64 decodes to the usual HCFG blob. Its header is `HCFG`, then u8 version (=1), u8 xor key, u16 length and u32 crc32, followed by single-byte-XOR'd JSON.\n- The JSON keys are campaign, domain, ipv4, url, mutex, windows_path, linux_path, service and host_model.\n- The \"DNS labels\" hint just points at the `domain`/`url` fields. There is no DNS wire-format encoding: the domain doesn't appear in plaintext anywhere in the binary (0 hits).\n\n**(2) Key insight:** this is the same HCFG family as Day 8 (Swift) and Day 9 (Nim), a base64 HCFG literal in rodata. `hcfg_offset` is the file offset of the base64 text itself, not of the Crystal String header 12 bytes before it.\n\n**(3) What worked:** `solver/solve.py` `native_hcfg()`:\n- Look for a raw `HCFG` first, then regex `rb\"SENGR[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]{12,}={0,2}\"`, pad, decode with `b64decode` or `urlsafe_b64decode`, and parse the result with `hcfg()`.\n- Set `offset = m.start()`.\n- `crc32_hex` is `f\"0x{zlib.crc32(plain):08x}\"`.\n- The format and machine fields come from pyelftools (`EM_X86_64` -> `x86-64`).\n- For a quick check by hand: `xxd -s 560176 -l 64 d13--*.elf`.\n\n**(4) Confirmed GM spellings (single_solve, attempt 62d7197b)**\n- `d13_artifact_sha256_1` = `6d141b21fba163c13dd065dea5b38cbd5d3f495fd8d0a15e1ef0a5c9d754e0df`\n- `d13_analysis_architecture` = `x86-64`; `d13_analysis_machine` = `x86-64`; `d13_analysis_format` = `elf`\n- `d13_analysis_config_campaign` = `BR13-000`\n- `d13_analysis_config_domain` = `node-000.day13.example.invalid`\n- `d13_analysis_config_mutex` = `Global\\HuntressBR13_000` (one backslash; it shows as `\\\\` in JSON)\n- `d13_analysis_config_url` = `https://node-000.day13.example.invalid/api/BR13-000`\n- `d13_analysis_config_crc32_hex` = `0xa69b7e61`\n- `d13_analysis_hcfg_offset` = `560188`\n- Rejected spellings: none. Each mode had exactly one submission, and each came back CORRECT.\n\n**(5) Pitfalls and time lost:** none on the solve itself; the worker only replied \"READY\". Two housekeeping problems:\n- `solver/NOTES.md` is stale. Its header reads \"binary-swift-justice solver notes\", and the `solve.py` docstring still says \"Day 07\". Neither mentions Day 13 or Crystal, so it's worth adding a line.\n- The `variant-*` cases (dated Oct 9) contain no d13 data, so they can't show the Day 13 differences.\n\n**(6) Start to CORRECT:** the attempt started at 13:00:08.619Z, the download landed at 13:00:14Z, and the GM recorded the solve at 13:00:22.916Z. That is about 14 s, with one probe submit of all 130 answers.\n\n**(7) Timed runs**\n\n| Mode | Attempt | Result | Time | Phases (start / download / solve / submit) | Answers |\n|---|---|---|-